---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const principles = [
  {
    n: '01',
    title: 'Abundance changes the bottleneck.',
    copy: 'Making one thing cheap does not make every constraint disappear. Cheap information can make attention more valuable. Cheap idea generation can make judgment, testing and implementation more important.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    title: 'More ideas are not the goal.',
    copy: 'The useful question is not how much a machine can generate. It is whether people become better able to understand, choose and build worthwhile things.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    title: 'Some scarcities can be reduced.',
    copy: 'Limits are real, but they are not all fixed. Knowledge, technology, infrastructure and better institutions can sometimes make an important constraint cheaper, easier to access or less important.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    title: 'Knowledge should become reusable capability.',
    copy: 'A result matters more when other people can inspect it, learn from it, reuse it or build on it. Open knowledge is therefore a strong default where privacy, security and other legitimate constraints allow it.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    title: 'Technology does not choose its own purpose.',
    copy: 'Who benefits from a capability depends on ownership, incentives, standards, institutions and the choices people make about how it is used.',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    title: 'Claims should survive contact with evidence.',
    copy: 'Interesting ideas are starting points. They should become narrower, change or disappear when prior work, experiments or criticism show that they should.',
  },
];
---
<Layout
  title="Manifesto"
  description="The Agalmic Research manifesto: make valuable capability easier to reach, while paying attention to the new bottlenecks abundance creates."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Manifesto</p>
      <h1>Make valuable capability easier to reach.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Agalmic Research starts from a simple idea: when knowledge and technology make something abundant, we should ask what becomes possible, what remains scarce, and how the new capability can be made genuinely useful.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell thesis">
      <p class="eyebrow">The direction</p>
      <blockquote>Abundance is not a world without limits. It is the repeated work of making worthwhile constraints less binding.</blockquote>
      <p>Some things will remain scarce. Time, attention, energy, expertise, trust, unique places and ecological capacity all impose real limits. But many constraints can change. The point of agalmic thinking is to notice which one is holding useful progress back now, and whether knowledge, technology or institutions can reduce it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Principles</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>Six ideas that guide the work.</h2>
          <p>These are directions for inquiry, not conclusions that research is required to defend.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        {principles.map((item) => (
          <article class="card">
            <span class="card-kicker">{item.n}</span>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.copy}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">What this means for research</p>
        <h2>Ideas are invitations to test, not territory to defend.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>When existing research already explains something well, use it. When an experiment contradicts a preferred idea, change the idea. When a tool works, make the useful part reusable where possible.</p>
        <p>Machine-generated arguments and polished drafts do not become true because they sound convincing. Evidence, criticism and appropriate expertise still matter.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="/research/">See the research</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="/results/">See what changed</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">A practical example</p>
        <h2>Representation changes what people can see.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><a href="https://nemosyne.world">Nemosyne</a> is an independent project that can test specific questions about how different representations of data affect understanding and discovery.</p>
        <p>That is the kind of connection Agalmic Research is interested in: take a broad idea about capability, turn it into something observable, and find out whether it actually helps.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell thesis">
      <p class="eyebrow">The aim</p>
      <blockquote>The goal is not more ideas. It is greater capacity to reach worthwhile futures.</blockquote>
      <p>This manifesto states a direction. The research pages show where that direction survives, changes or fails.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
